00:06:16.711 --> 00:06:19.090
Recently, there was a
new Google Cloud Platform

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.510
region that has started--

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:22.784
that has been brought
up in Sydney, Australia.

00:06:22.784 --> 00:06:24.700
And I sort of wanted to
talk about this impact

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.760
that that has had for both of
you in the areas in which you

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:28.840
work in Australia.

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:31.400
I thought I'd start maybe by
taking a step back a little,

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.782
and one of you can choose--

00:06:32.782 --> 00:06:34.240
I don't know which
one-- but I want

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:37.270
to explain the current internet
situation in Australia.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:40.500
It's a little less than ideal
for a variety of reasons.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.650
Who wants to take this one?

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.225
I just wanted to get
some background there.

00:06:43.225 --> 00:06:45.183
ANDREW WALKER: Yeah,
look, I'd love to do that.

00:06:45.183 --> 00:06:47.710
So I guess the biggest
thing about Australia

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:49.660
is our remoteness.

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.490
People who are really
closely connected,

00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:53.710
like in Europe and
America, are used

00:06:53.710 --> 00:06:56.660
to having high speeds of
connectivity between point A

00:06:56.660 --> 00:07:00.560
and point B. So, you know,
for the last 10 years,

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:03.940
we've been dealing
with round trip times

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.770
to either the west
coast of America,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.505
or somewhere up in Asia,
or somewhere in Europe.

00:07:08.505 --> 00:07:09.880
So we've had to
work around that.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:11.710
And, I guess, the
second thing is

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:16.975
that our infrastructure here
in Australia is not great.

00:07:16.975 --> 00:07:19.330
In the lead up to
the conversation,

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:20.830
Graham was saying
that he's actually

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:23.881
connecting over 4G just
to make this podcast,

00:07:23.881 --> 00:07:25.380
and I'm doing exactly
the same thing

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:29.227
because my internet connection
at home is just so poor.

00:07:29.227 --> 00:07:31.810
So that's something that we've
had to work around things a lot

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:34.502
for the last few years while we
build solutions for enterprise.

00:07:34.502 --> 00:07:35.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Interesting.

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:38.500
So you were mentioning
that this is--

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.230
in your houses-- the
connectivity is not that good,

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:43.720
but what about the office?

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:46.240
As a developer, is this
something that changes

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:48.790
the way you write
code, specifically

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:50.080
cloud solutions and stuff?

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.380
ANDREW WALKER: So we do
everything in the cloud.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.830
We're an asset-less company.

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:55.300
We're a serverless company.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:59.170
So connectivity is huge for us.

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.227
Normally at offices it's fine.

00:08:01.227 --> 00:08:02.810
I say normally, like
we'll get outages

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.300
during the middle of the
day sometimes at our office,

00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:07.930
and then we all switch
to back-up on 4G.

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.180
So as a company
our biggest expense

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.460
outside airfares
and entertainment

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.545
is actually internet
connectivity.

00:08:14.545 --> 00:08:16.760
MARK MANDEL: So I guess
then, especially with you,

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:18.465
Graham, you work
in big data sets,

00:08:18.465 --> 00:08:19.840
you're not pulling
down terabytes

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:21.475
of data backwards and forwards?

00:08:21.475 --> 00:08:22.970
GRAHAM POLLEY: Well, no.

00:08:22.970 --> 00:08:26.080
A common pattern that
I normally employ

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:28.470
is actually bring
the code to the data,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.100
not the data to the code--

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:34.630
so using tools and
services like BigQuery,

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:38.500
where that data is stored
currently in the US.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:40.559
Writing the SQL
and UDF functions

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:42.190
for that is a much
better pattern,

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.890
rather than downloading massive
data sets and working locally,

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:47.200
or spinning up other
VMs and working there.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:49.960
One of the big changes for
me with the new Sydney region

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:51.670
is, although BigQuery
is not there yet,

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:53.260
we have data flow in region.

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.650
So customers are
a lot happier now.

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:58.240
They're closer to the data.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.680
They feel more secure.

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.380
So that data can be
processed and stored locally

00:09:02.380 --> 00:09:03.920
in Australia, and
that's fantastic.

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.640
So we're spinning up Dataflow
pipelines in region there.

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.950
And that's a game
changer for us,

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:11.530
because it makes those
conversations a lot easier

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.060
to have with enterprises.

00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:14.740
Especially their
security operations

00:09:14.740 --> 00:09:17.560
and privacy, because they feel
more comfortable that the data

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:18.787
is now stored locally.

00:09:18.787 --> 00:09:21.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm curious
about what you were mentioning

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.900
about locality of data.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.690
Is there specific
Australian policies

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:28.120
that make it that
you'd need to have

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.640
the data stored in the country?

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.460
Or is it more about people
just feeling more comfortable

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:34.075
about it?

00:09:34.075 --> 00:09:35.910
GRAHAM POLLEY:
There's actually laws,

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:38.300
and it's around
data sovereignty.

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:42.580
So if any of the data
has PII information-- so

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.770
Personally Identifiable
Information--

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:46.930
that can't be shipped offshore.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:48.682
That has to stay in Australia.

00:09:48.682 --> 00:09:50.890
And that's a challenge for
us, and that's always been

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:52.910
a challenge for us in the past.

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:54.730
So that's what I mean
with the new data

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:56.980
center, or the new region
coming online in Sydney.

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:58.990
It makes those
conversations a lot easier.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:00.490
We've worked around
that in the past

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:02.630
by applying caching to the data.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.790
So that means we don't
have any plain text

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:08.810
PII in our analytics
inside our data.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:13.050
So that's gotten us around
those challenges with security

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:14.820
and privacy in the past.

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:18.210
So having the new region
in Sydney, and especially

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:21.180
with Dataflow, it just means
that those conversations

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.370
are a lot easier to
have, because the data

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:24.395
is stored locally.

00:10:24.395 --> 00:10:27.090
MARK MANDEL: Andrew, have you
had similar situations, where

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.520
before the region spun up
there were certain work arounds

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.520
you had to put in place?

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:32.364
ANDREW WALKER: Yeah,
look, our experience

00:10:32.364 --> 00:10:33.530
has been slightly different.

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.950
We do less work in
government and finance,

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:40.580
mainly because they have some
very specific legislation,

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.040
specifically in PII
that holds them back.

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:45.750
We've had less
constraint, because we

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.270
avoid those in the
main, that we have

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:51.410
done things like administer
de-briefing systems in Google

00:10:51.410 --> 00:10:52.660
Cloud before.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:57.240
So really the core issue
for us has typically

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.360
been away from sovereignty
and more towards privacy

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:01.410
in most sectors.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:04.950
And it hasn't been such a
large theoretical issue,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:08.170
but actually, the theoretical
issues is sort of irrelevant.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:09.900
Having data in
Australia just means

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.260
you don't have six months
of lead time toward a job.

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.080
That's as simple as it gets
for us-- having the region here

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.746
just means there is 10
conversations with 10

00:11:18.746 --> 00:11:21.120
separate groups of people that
we just don't have to have

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.910
because they're not confused.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:26.190
So I can give you probably
three examples of projects

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.080
that we're doing recently
here in Australia.

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:30.750
One for a big utility
company, for Fieldforce

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:35.820
in field engineers, another
one in retail, another one in--

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.460
actually, ironically
in the finance sector.

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:41.650
All of which have kicked
off a lot more quickly,

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.800
because they haven't had
to have those discussions

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.650
around privacy in particular.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:47.790
And we've had to employ
the same sorts of issues

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.910
even in web solutions that
Graham was talking about.

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:53.190
So we've had to make sure
that we spend a little bit

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:55.020
more time on premise--

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:56.550
preparing data
before it goes out.

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.620
It creates some web
services for things

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.945
that we don't want to store
in the Cloud and so on.

00:12:00.945 --> 00:12:05.612
And all of those just disappear
for everything except big data.

00:12:05.612 --> 00:12:08.070
That whole problem has just
gone away for us on App Engine.

00:12:08.070 --> 00:12:08.986
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:12:08.986 --> 00:12:11.070
So I'm wondering now--

00:12:11.070 --> 00:12:13.620
you mentioned that one of
things that have been solved

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.960
is the locality, and the
fact that the data doesn't

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:19.200
leave the country, which
solves a series of problems.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:22.530
Is there any other things
that the fact that Google

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:25.650
opened a new cloud computing
region in Australia

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:26.400
has fixed for you?

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.233
ANDREW WALKER: Yeah.

00:12:27.233 --> 00:12:29.012
A good example of
this is a big project

00:12:29.012 --> 00:12:30.720
we did for one of the
media players here.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.330
Where we opened up
all of the content

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:35.520
for basically all
of their digital

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:40.560
and actually newspaper estates,
right across like 150 estates.

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:43.600
Back in those days latency
was a really big issue for us.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:45.570
We've been able to avoid
latency as an issue

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.780
when it comes down to user
facing apps on App Engine,

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.050
because the latency of
the quarter of a second,

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:53.520
or a fifth of a second
wasn't something

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.322
that really had to be taken into
account for user experience.

00:12:56.322 --> 00:12:57.780
But when you're
building an API you

00:12:57.780 --> 00:13:01.390
need 30 milliseconds, 50
millisecond response times.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:03.420
So we've had to use
Amazon in situations

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:04.770
like that in the past.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:07.691
We do that project again today,
and I get to do that on GCP

00:13:07.691 --> 00:13:08.190
now.

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.523
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah, I think
the latency, the round trip

00:13:10.523 --> 00:13:12.560
latency, is an
important one as well.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:15.780
So on average, in
the past, I'd be

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:17.910
getting maybe 250,
300 milliseconds,

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:21.340
so now I'm seeing 10
to 15 milliseconds,

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:23.440
which is fantastic.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.850
Yeah, you can go to-- do you
guys know the GCP ping website?

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:28.410
It's really cool.

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.840
The GCP ping, and now
for the Sydney region

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.700
I'm seeing even sometimes
below 10 milliseconds.

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:34.325
It's fantastic.

00:13:34.325 --> 00:13:36.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Nice, that is awesome.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.670
So, Andrew, you
mentioned that you

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:41.910
were using Amazon for some
other things, and that is true.

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:44.160
Before Google
Cloud had a region,

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:45.750
Amazon was already there.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:47.610
So why using Google Cloud?

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:51.650
Is there any benefit or
advantage that made you use it?

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:53.450
ANDREW WALKER: Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.124
We're fast becoming
100% GCP, especially

00:13:56.124 --> 00:13:57.790
with the region opening
up in Australia.

00:13:57.790 --> 00:13:59.550
And that's not because--

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:01.380
you know, we're
actually not resellers.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:04.290
We make our money out of
building stuff for people.

00:14:04.290 --> 00:14:06.060
So it really comes
down to money.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:10.020
We had a client onstage at the
Google Cloud Summit explaining

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.320
just how much cheaper
it is to build on GCP,

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:15.180
and it really just comes
down to serverless.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:19.170
GCP is genuinely serverless,
whereas Amazon never really

00:14:19.170 --> 00:14:20.670
has been-- not in
a practical sense.

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:25.140
They have their equivalent
to Cloud Functions,

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:28.054
but they don't have an
equivalent to App Engine.

00:14:28.054 --> 00:14:29.970
With the richness of
services that that gives,

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:32.010
it's just such an
exciting technology.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:36.290
And I do also agree with
Graham that BigQuery

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.170
and supporting
services is just--

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:39.930
it's got to be the crown jewel.

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:42.870
We get 50% of our
speed benefits.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:46.090
We generally move 50%
faster than anyone else.

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:49.050
We usually deliver
somewhere between a quarter

00:14:49.050 --> 00:14:52.860
to a half of the budget
of our competitors.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:56.100
And 50% of that speed
advantage comes just

00:14:56.100 --> 00:14:58.290
from the serverless
cloud, and that's really

00:14:58.290 --> 00:14:59.780
what GCP is for us.

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:02.040
We have the whole
deploy on Monday thing.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:04.404
So we're not spending
four weeks as we

00:15:04.404 --> 00:15:06.070
have done on some of
our Amazon projects

00:15:06.070 --> 00:15:08.414
just waiting, having discussions
around infrastructure.

00:15:08.414 --> 00:15:10.330
OK, it might be in the
cloud, but you're still

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:12.620
engineering it, which
is really painful when

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.712
you have to go back to that
from a serverless world.

00:15:15.712 --> 00:15:18.860
The scale to zero thing
as quoted by our customers

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.400
who have got 200 environments.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:23.530
The fact that so many
of those scale to zero

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:27.180
makes a massive
operational cost impact.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:30.110
We're talking in some cases
10% of the cost of the Amazon

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.840
infrastructure.

00:15:31.840 --> 00:15:34.360
So these things make
a massive difference.

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:36.850
So I guess the one final thing
that gives us a huge speed

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:40.000
difference in just
development is

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:43.570
the local emulation on the SDK.

00:15:43.570 --> 00:15:45.460
So the ability for
a developer to run

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:48.110
locally without having
to be their own DevOps

00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:51.460
person, without having
to setup Tomcat,

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:54.350
without having to setup
MySQL on the local machine.

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:56.440
It's really hard
to overestimate.

00:15:56.440 --> 00:15:59.740
It's such a phenomenal thing,
and one of the key things

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:02.005
that brings us back to
App Engine every time.

00:16:02.005 --> 00:16:04.900
MARK MANDEL: Out of curiosity,
because I'm actually

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:06.920
surprised Francesc
hasn't asked, Andrew,

00:16:06.920 --> 00:16:10.450
what language are you
writing on App Engine?

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.080
ANDREW WALKER: So
we write in Java.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.740
We're not particularly
wedded to Java,

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.230
but we choose one language
across the company

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.190
to give us that scale.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.975
We like type safety,
so we've chosen Java,

00:16:21.975 --> 00:16:24.980
and that killer feature of--

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:27.520
I think, it's only about three
languages that support it--

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.340
of having the local emulation,
100% local likely emulation.

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:33.520
Even with task queues, you
know of all the key elements

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:36.360
of the things that we're
using on an everyday basis.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:39.610
So one day we'll probably
move to Node as the backend

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:42.640
when we get type safety,
and that works well for us.

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:45.449
But for the moment most of the
projects we do are in Java.

00:16:45.449 --> 00:16:47.740
GRAHAM POLLEY: I think another
thing worth pointing out

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:49.750
is per second billing now.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:50.500
That's huge.

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:52.156
Right, so that was
announced last week,

00:16:52.156 --> 00:16:53.530
so we now have
per second billing

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:55.117
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:55.117 --> 00:16:57.200
That's going to save our
customers a lot of money,

00:16:57.200 --> 00:17:00.970
especially for me writing
Dataflow jobs and batch

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:04.660
pipelines, and that saves
our customers and clients

00:17:04.660 --> 00:17:05.710
a lot of money.

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:06.714
It's fantastic.

00:17:06.714 --> 00:17:07.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:17:07.630 --> 00:17:11.290
So, Graham, something that you
mentioned a couple, or even

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:14.599
a bunch of times, was the
fact that there's no BigQuery.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:16.839
So let's talk about that.

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:19.319
GRAHAM POLLEY: I love
talking about BigQuery.

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.609
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I know, I know.

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:24.800
So tell us a little bit not
only why you want BigQuery--

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.520
I assume that
BigQuery is important.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.680
But since BigQuery is not there,
but you are a Google Developer

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:35.380
Expert partly because of the
job that you do BigQuery, what

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:38.440
is your experience using
BigQuery from a region that

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:39.725
doesn't have it yet?

00:17:39.725 --> 00:17:42.530
GRAHAM POLLEY: OK, so using
BigQuery is a no-brainer.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:46.180
It's just a phenomenal product,
and Andrew touched on it

00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:46.900
already.

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:48.960
For me, and I think
Andrew will agree with me,

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.870
it's the jewel in the crown
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.410
There's no product like it.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.260
It's the speed at which
you can grind true data.

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:57.430
It's the scale.

00:17:57.430 --> 00:17:58.810
It's the ease of use.

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:01.030
It's got a good REST API.

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:02.860
It's now got standard SQL.

00:18:02.860 --> 00:18:04.840
It's just a fantastic product.

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:09.040
We've been using
BigQuery since 2012,

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:12.110
2013, so quite a few years.

00:18:12.110 --> 00:18:16.810
And we've been in Telstra,
which is Australia's largest

00:18:16.810 --> 00:18:18.275
ISP in TeleCom.

00:18:18.275 --> 00:18:20.650
And we've worked around the
issues of not having BigQuery

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:22.950
in region, and I
touched on this earlier,

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.940
by hashing a lot of
the PII information--

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.330
or actually just stripping
it out completely.

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:30.610
That keeps their security
and privacy teams happy.

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.900
There's another little
trick that you can do,

00:18:32.900 --> 00:18:35.080
and maybe not a lot of
people know about this,

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:38.130
and you will take a
hit on performance.

00:18:38.130 --> 00:18:41.260
But what you can actually
do is you can still

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:44.170
store your data locally.

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.350
So what you could do, because
we have the region in Sydney,

00:18:47.350 --> 00:18:50.500
you could store
your data in GCS--

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:51.980
so locally in region--

00:18:51.980 --> 00:18:54.490
and use federated
sources in BigQuery.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:57.320
Because BigQuery does
everything in memory,

00:18:57.320 --> 00:19:00.700
so it's gonna pull that
data, churn through it

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:03.880
in memory in either
the EU or the US.

00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:07.120
But that data is
not stored locally,

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:10.960
so it's only being
processed offshore.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:14.590
Now whether that gets
through security constraints,

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:17.410
and it's something
that enterprises

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.270
are comfortable with,
I don't know yet.

00:19:20.270 --> 00:19:24.220
I haven't tested it out
with large enterprises yet,

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:28.510
but it's a little trick
that maybe people can employ

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:30.370
to get around those issues.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:34.210
But I think the
massive benefit would

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.430
be getting BigQuery in region.

00:19:36.430 --> 00:19:39.400
So the benefit there would
be a lot more enterprise,

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:41.740
and government,
and customers would

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:44.770
be willing to adopt BigQuery
into their workloads

00:19:44.770 --> 00:19:46.240
if they knew it was in region.

00:19:46.240 --> 00:19:49.210
And that, again, comes
down to having PII--

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:50.911
it's all about PII--

00:19:50.911 --> 00:19:53.410
and if it was in region they
would be a lot more comfortable

00:19:53.410 --> 00:19:54.350
with that.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:58.180
So fingers crossed if we
can build enough use cases

00:19:58.180 --> 00:20:00.680
and show Google the value of
having BigQuery in region,

00:20:00.680 --> 00:20:04.040
and maybe it will come
online in the near future.

00:20:04.040 --> 00:20:06.229
So I'm holding out for that one.

00:20:06.229 --> 00:20:07.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Everything I can say

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:10.080
is that the product
managers for BigQuery,

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.962
they do listen to the podcast.

00:20:12.962 --> 00:20:14.420
GRAHAM POLLEY:
That's good to hear.

00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:15.330
That's good to hear.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:16.940
ANDREW WALKER: So, guys,
I wouldn't mind also

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:18.220
adding a little bit to that.

00:20:18.220 --> 00:20:19.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, go for it.

00:20:19.310 --> 00:20:21.730
ANDREW WALKER: I would agree
with everything Graham says.

00:20:21.730 --> 00:20:24.630
I love BigQuery for the
bigness of BigQuery.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.680
I think that one of the other
strengths of the platform that

00:20:27.680 --> 00:20:30.650
gets overlooked a
lot, actually, is

00:20:30.650 --> 00:20:32.797
that we have this phrase
in three weeks that says,

00:20:32.797 --> 00:20:33.880
"Serverless means Monday."

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:39.010
The fact that I can get
the stuff up and running,

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:41.320
and literally get like
a terabyte of data

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:44.720
up, on Monday morning,
and incorporate this

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:49.575
into a solution into some AI ML.

00:20:49.575 --> 00:20:52.200
Even just the simplest thing of
just treating it as a reporting

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.330
engine is amazingly cool.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:55.970
When you can stand
that thing up--

00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.090
and it's been stood
up, in fact, for you--

00:20:58.090 --> 00:21:00.350
it's just part of your
project and is immediately

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:02.190
available to your project.

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:03.950
It's also easy to
underestimate that.

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:06.440
So normally if I'm having
a conversation with someone

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.430
about how to report
on something,

00:21:08.430 --> 00:21:10.600
again, it's a binary thing.

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:13.620
It's not, oh, it'll
add a couple of days.

00:21:13.620 --> 00:21:16.540
If there's some
non-serverless element then

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:17.750
it's going to add months.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:20.240
It adds like two months to
talk about it, two months

00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:21.830
to agree on something.

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:25.680
So the fact that we
use BigQuery by default

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:28.387
as our standard
reporting engine means

00:21:28.387 --> 00:21:30.470
that we can provide a whole
bunch of functionality

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:34.882
around, say, a mobile solution,
or around a web based solution.

00:21:34.882 --> 00:21:36.590
That you wouldn't be
able to do if you're

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:40.140
having to setup a local, or
even a partially local solution.

00:21:40.140 --> 00:21:43.180
So I would also say that that
is a big factor for bringing

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.527
BigQuery to Australia.

00:21:45.527 --> 00:21:47.610
At the moment, if I do
have to work around things,

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:50.430
if do have to process
data in some way,

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:53.370
if I have to remove
keys internally,

00:21:53.370 --> 00:21:55.730
I immediately have to involve
a whole bunch of people

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:57.020
who are resource constrained.

00:21:57.020 --> 00:21:59.930
And as soon as I do
that, I'm into months.

00:21:59.930 --> 00:22:02.450
We had a recent project
at the University

00:22:02.450 --> 00:22:04.770
where we just had to make
a small change exactly

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:06.804
like what Graham was
describing, but we

00:22:06.804 --> 00:22:09.095
had to wait for a team of
people to do that internally,

00:22:09.095 --> 00:22:12.050
and it took literally four
weeks just to talk to them.

00:22:12.050 --> 00:22:14.710
So that whole
"serverless means Monday"

00:22:14.710 --> 00:22:16.720
thing I couldn't overemphasize.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.640
It's so cool being able to
stand it up immediately.

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:20.370
We write code.

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:21.160
We deploy Monday.

00:22:21.160 --> 00:22:22.190
We report Tuesday.

00:22:22.190 --> 00:22:24.920
Customers use it on Wednesday
to do real reporting.

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:27.660
And I think the final thing
there is the Data Studio.

00:22:27.660 --> 00:22:31.190
As Data Studio
matures, that is going

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:33.800
to be such a cool tool for
us and for our clients.

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.000
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:22:35.000 --> 00:22:38.630
So I guess looking forward
I'll be kind of curious to hear

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:40.767
how that now there is
a region in Sydney,

00:22:40.767 --> 00:22:43.100
how has that changed the plans
for what you're planning,

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:45.256
develop looking forward.

00:22:45.256 --> 00:22:47.964
ANDREW WALKER: The biggest
change for us is that--

00:22:47.964 --> 00:22:49.380
if I could put it
really bluntly--

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:52.580
is we're going to let our
partnership with Amazon lapse.

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:55.190
Been doing less and less work,
and since the Sydney region

00:22:55.190 --> 00:22:58.520
came on, it's hard to
imagine a situation where

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:00.770
we would put our
hand on heart say

00:23:00.770 --> 00:23:03.334
that we're doing the right
thing with our client's money.

00:23:03.334 --> 00:23:05.500
By doubling the budget, I
mean that's literally what

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:06.791
we're going to be doing, right.

00:23:06.791 --> 00:23:09.350
So for us, the most
fundamental change

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:12.930
is that we're going to sort of
become de facto until there's

00:23:12.930 --> 00:23:15.820
another serverless offering like
App Engine and the supporting

00:23:15.820 --> 00:23:16.910
tools.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:18.117
So that's part of it.

00:23:18.117 --> 00:23:19.700
But the other part
is that we're going

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:22.200
to go after some of the
clients that we weren't that

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.325
excited about, and I think
government is a good one

00:23:24.325 --> 00:23:25.670
that Graham mentioned.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:27.400
We've done some great
work in government,

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:28.950
but they just didn't--

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:30.845
I guess, scared of the cloud.

00:23:30.845 --> 00:23:33.070
And in some cases
not rightly so-- but

00:23:33.070 --> 00:23:34.820
it doesn't matter if
they're still scared.

00:23:34.820 --> 00:23:37.460
So that makes a big difference
for us in terms of the sectors

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:38.570
that we are going after.

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:40.530
Government comes back
onto the radar for us.

00:23:40.530 --> 00:23:41.850
MARK MANDEL: And you, Graham?

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:44.230
GRAHAM POLLEY: Yeah,
for me at least,

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:48.710
and for working for Shine, I can
see the momentum growing now.

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:52.124
So it's really gaining
traction, especially

00:23:52.124 --> 00:23:53.540
