00:28:40.810 --> 00:28:43.240
This week we are talking
about TensorFlow.

00:28:43.240 --> 00:28:46.180
Now, I don't know a huge
amount about TensorFlow,

00:28:46.180 --> 00:28:48.730
but we got a question in from
one of our wonderful listeners.

00:28:48.730 --> 00:28:51.130
I'm going to mispronounce
his name most likely,

00:28:51.130 --> 00:28:53.600
Sy-mohn A-kuh-shee-nuh,
possibly?

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:54.235
A-kuh-chee-nuh?

00:28:54.235 --> 00:28:55.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm
going to say it's See-mohn

00:28:55.530 --> 00:28:57.370
A-kuh-chee-nuh or
A-kuh-chee-nuh.

00:28:57.370 --> 00:28:58.000
MARK MANDEL: You're probably--

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm not very
sure, but something like that.

00:28:59.740 --> 00:29:00.930
MARK MANDEL: I'll just
somehow Australianify it.

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:01.842
It'll be fine.

00:29:01.842 --> 00:29:03.550
But he asks an
interesting question about

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.820
whether TensorFlow is good
for general math computation,

00:29:06.820 --> 00:29:09.700
like doing linear algebra,
or even just adding stuff

00:29:09.700 --> 00:29:10.210
together.

00:29:10.210 --> 00:29:12.210
Can you use it for that
kind of stuff, Francesc?

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:14.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So the
quick answer is, yes,

00:29:14.440 --> 00:29:15.962
and it totally makes sense.

00:29:15.962 --> 00:29:17.920
Because if you think
about it, Machine Learning

00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:22.630
is basically about matrix
multiplication, mostly.

00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:26.800
Some functions on top, but it's
mostly matrix multiplication.

00:29:26.800 --> 00:29:30.520
And linear algebra is also very
often a matrix multiplication,

00:29:30.520 --> 00:29:32.050
or operations of a matrix--

00:29:32.050 --> 00:29:34.290
like the product,
and multiplication

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:37.030
and decomposition,
or stuff like that.

00:29:37.030 --> 00:29:40.030
So it totally makes
sense that if something

00:29:40.030 --> 00:29:42.100
is good for Machine
Learning, it should be

00:29:42.100 --> 00:29:44.560
pretty good for linear algebra.

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:47.200
Now, the cool thing
that I didn't expect

00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:50.560
is that if you compare
TensorFlow versus NumPy,

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:53.029
TensorFlow is incredibly
faster than NumPy.

00:29:53.029 --> 00:29:54.070
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:29:54.070 --> 00:29:55.486
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And I am not sure

00:29:55.486 --> 00:29:58.519
if it is because TensorFlow
uses the GPU, which it does,

00:29:58.519 --> 00:30:01.060
unless you're running it on a
MacBook with the latest version

00:30:01.060 --> 00:30:03.180
of TensorFlow which, reasons.

00:30:03.180 --> 00:30:05.440
But in general
you're going to get

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.920
incredibly faster computations,
just because you're

00:30:07.920 --> 00:30:09.450
using all the GPU.

00:30:09.450 --> 00:30:12.960
And I will add a link
to a specific article

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:15.730
where they compare NumPy,
to Theano, to TensorFlow--

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:17.790
Theano is a different
library that

00:30:17.790 --> 00:30:20.680
provides similar things
to TensorFlow really.

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:23.040
And you will see that
both Theano and TensorFlow

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:27.240
are almost exponentially faster
than NumPy for most operations.

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:29.760
It is pretty amazing
for things like finding

00:30:29.760 --> 00:30:32.260
the minimum, or the sum,
and stuff like that.

00:30:32.260 --> 00:30:34.500
TensorFlow is basically
the whole line

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:37.830
is basically flat
on how long it takes

00:30:37.830 --> 00:30:40.680
to find the sum of a matrix.

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:43.560
No matter if it's 500
elements or 5,000 elements,

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:46.110
it's still pretty much the
same, which is really good.

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:50.760
So if you're doing linear
algebra I'd say try TensorFlow.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:51.990
It is really simple--

00:30:51.990 --> 00:30:54.270
most operations
have the same name--

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:56.010
both NumPy and TensorFlow.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:59.424
So it's not very hard to move
from one way to the other.

00:30:59.424 --> 00:31:00.840
And at the end
you're going to get

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:03.015
code that executes way faster--

00:31:03.015 --> 00:31:04.140
so definitely worth trying.

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:05.014
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:31:05.014 --> 00:31:06.835
Well, that sounds
good, and thank you

00:31:06.835 --> 00:31:07.960
for providing that answer--

00:31:07.960 --> 00:31:08.850
I learned something new today.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:09.990
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Then
you're very welcome.

00:31:09.990 --> 00:31:10.560
MARK MANDEL: That's good.

